Ruby 4.0 in WebAssembly without Asyncify, for the plugin's module for engines with JSPI, JavaScript Promise
Integration, which scripts/ruby_wasm.rb builds with this with --jspi, rather than the jumps patch, with the shapes
patch, and ruby-4.0-jspi-idle-gc.patch, from a wasi-sdk with LLVM 19 or later and Binaryen 131, which bin/build
downloads.

Asyncify instruments most of Ruby's C so it can unwind and rewind the stack, for setjmp, for garbage collection to read
its locals, and to switch Fibers, which makes all of it slower, about 1.5 to 2 times, and the module bigger. Built
with RB_WASM_EH, and with -mllvm -wasm-enable-sjlj -mexception-handling and -lsetjmp:

1. setjmp is wasi-libc's, from WebAssembly exception handling, in eval_intern.h, vm_core.h and gc.c, and vm_exec takes
   Ruby's own path with it, in vm.c, with a jmp_buf of the usual size, in vm_core.h.
2. Garbage collection scans only the stack in memory, in gc.c, and can't see WebAssembly's locals, as the configure
   runs Binaryen's --flatten and --coalesce-locals after linking, in place of --asyncify, but not --spill-pointers,
   which would keep every value that's live across a call on the stack there, at a cost of 15 to 25%. So
   ruby-4.0-jspi-idle-gc.patch only collects between requests, with no Ruby on the stack.
3. Each Fiber is a stack of JSPI's, in wasm/fiber.c: rb_wasm_jspi.switch, a WebAssembly.Suspending the worker gives,
   suspends the stack until something switches back to it, and resumes the one switched to, or starts a new one's by
   calling rb_wasm_jspi_fiber_start through WebAssembly.promising. So Fibers switch in Ruby the worker calls through
   WebAssembly.promising, like rb_wasm_jspi_eval, in wasm/missing.c, which runs RbWasmJSPI.run.
4. Ruby waits for JavaScript with RbWasmJSPI.wait, in wasm/missing.c, which rb_wasm_jspi_define defines, calling
   rb_wasm_jspi.await, a WebAssembly.Suspending, until the promise the worker keeps by its id settles.
5. Asyncify's functions are wasm/missing.c's, in wasm/asyncify.h, which trap, but for stopping an unwind, which nothing
   starts. The js gem's extension imports them, and links to these.

